---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SuiteToggle from '../components/SuiteToggle.astro';
import ExportButtons from '../components/ExportButtons.astro';
---
<Base title="Peira frontier, robustness against cost and latency" current="Frontier" ogImage="og-frontier.png">
  <div data-page="frontier">
    <div class="wrap">
      <section class="hero">
        <div class="kicker">Cost and latency frontier</div>
        <h1>Robustness <span class="amber">per dollar</span> and per millisecond</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          A guardrail that never flips but costs a fortune is a different product than a
          cheap one that flips half the time. This view plots conditional attack success
          rate against list-price cost, with bubble area showing p99 latency.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="controls">
        <SuiteToggle />
        <label><input type="checkbox" id="logx" checked /> Log scale on the cost axis</label>
      </div>

      <div class="chart-wrap">
        <div class="chart-scroll" id="frontier-chart"></div>
        <p class="hint" id="frontier-note"></p>
      </div>

      <ExportButtons svg={true} png={true} />

      <section class="prose">
        <h2>How to read this view</h2>
        <p>
          The best tradeoffs sit toward the bottom left. An adapter is on the frontier when no
          other adapter is both cheaper and more robust. Cost is list-price USD per 1,000
          decisions from the pricing table in effect at run time.
        </p>
        <p class="note">
          Cost data appears only when run artifacts include priced usage records.
          Mock runs carry no pricing table, so the frontier is empty on mock builds.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
